data.content:
Your code **doesn't use `Thread.Abort`** at all. It only uses `Thread.Start()`, `Thread.Join()`, and `Thread.Sleep()` — all of which work identically on .NET 8. No migration is needed for this code.

That said, for .NET 8 I'd recommend modernizing to `Parallel.ForEach` or `Task`-based concurrency:

```csharp
public class SimpleWorker
{
    public void RunBatch(List<WorkItem> items)
    {
        Parallel.ForEach(items, item =>
        {
            Thread.Sleep(100);
            item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
        });
    }
}
```

This gives you thread pool efficiency and built-in `CancellationToken` support if you need it later — but it's an improvement, not a required migration.
